  "text": "When high‑frequency compressional and shear acoustic pulses travel through a polycrystalline metal bar, part of their energy disappears because the grains scatter and diffuse the waves. If the average grain dimension is smaller than about one‑third of the acoustic wavelength, the loss follows a fourth‑power dependence on frequency and scales with the total grain volume. The strength of this scattering is set by how much the material’s stiffness varies with direction, giving separate coefficients for compressional and shear modes. These coefficients have been worked out for two typical crystal symmetries. Using measured stiffness data, only a few metals—a lightweight alloy, a low‑density metal, and a dense refractory metal—show very small losses. Calculations predict similar compressional losses for the first two, but the low‑density metal has an especially tiny shear loss, comparable to that of ordinary glass. At still higher frequencies, when the wavelength becomes comparable to or shorter than the grain size, the wave propagation turns into a diffusion‑like process, analogous to heat flow. The grain size then fixes the average distance between scattering events, making the loss inversely proportional to grain diameter. An approximate expression for these diffusion losses matches the observed values closely."
